2,Nyungar alleluia : SA choir with piano by Dan Walker : Work : Australian Music Centre,https://www.australianmusiccentre.com.au/workversion/walker-dan-nyungar-alleluia/29372,"Nyungar alleluia : SA choir with piano by Dan Walker - find sheet music, recordings, digital score and audio samples, analysis, performances and more at the Australian Music Centre.",Nyungar,Australian
3,Indigenous music of Australia — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Indigenous_music_of_Australia,"January 14, 2026 - In the early 20th century, Indigenous Australian musicians adapted European instruments such as the violin to sustain cultural practices amid assimilation policies that suppressed traditional ceremonies. For instance, Nyungar man Peter Jetta (c.",Nyungar,Australian
4,Score: Nyungar alleluia / words and music by Dan Walker. [2014] : Product : Australian Music Centre,https://www.australianmusiccentre.com.au/product/nyungar-alleluia,A short uplifting work about the beauty and spirituality of the Australian landscape. The Nyungar people are the traditional people of South West Western Australia. This piece would suit a good primary or middle school choir. Published by: Australian Music Centre — 1 facsimile score (7p.,Nyungar,Australian
5,How Aboriginal musicians enhanced and maintained community in 20th century Australia - The University of Sydney,https://www.sydney.edu.au/news-opinion/news/2024/07/15/how-aboriginal-musicians-enhanced-and-maintained-community-in-20th-century-australia-music-expert.html,"Aboriginal people used music in the creation and preservation of individual, cultural and collective identities. The violin was used on their own terms. Peter Jetta, photographed for the Western Mail, 1933.",Nyungar,Australian
6,Reigniting the Noongar language through song | Australian Research Council,https://www.arc.gov.au/news-publications/media/feature-articles/reigniting-noongar-language-through-song,"September 18, 2018 - Dr Clint Bracknell is a musician and ethnomusicologist, based at the Sydney Conservatorium of Music at The University of Sydney, who is using an Australian Research Council (ARC) Discovery Indigenous grant to bring to life the musical traditions of the Noongar language of South Western Australia.",Nyungar,Australian
7,‘Say You’re a Nyungarmusicologist’: Indigenous Research and Endangered Song,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/297673630_'Say_You're_a_Nyungarmusicologist'_Indigenous_Research_and_Endangered_Song,"July 3, 2015 - Based on my work as an Aboriginal Australian researcher studying song traditions in the Southwest of Western Australia, this article interrogates notions of ‘native’ music researchers and distinctly Indigenous approaches to research.",Nyungar,Australian
8,Conceptualizing Noongar Song | Yearbook for Traditional Music | Cambridge Core,https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/yearbook-for-traditional-music/article/abs/conceptualizing-noongar-song/27870617C7095DA474D5AC705F8AA442,"December 6, 2018 - As of 2011, an estimated 669,900 ... (Australian Bureau of Statistics 2015). Of that population, over 30,000 people from a uniquely large urban/rural area in the southwest of Western Australia—including the author of this article—identify as Noongar (also spelled Nyungar)...",Nyungar,Australian
9,"‘Say You’re a Nyungarmusicologist’: Indigenous Research and Endangered Song: Musicology Australia: Vol 37, No 2",https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08145857.2015.1075260,"His research explores links between song and language, emerging technologies, and Indigenous creative futures. A musician and composer, he was nominated for ‘Best Original Score’ in the 2012 Helpmann Awards. His Nyungar cultural elders use the term ‘Wirlomin’ to refer to their clan.",Nyungar,Australian
